The legal texts come from official publications of the Republic of Mauritius:

  The Constitution of Mauritius in the Revised Laws of Mauritius, Attorney General's Office
  (https://attorneygeneral.govmu.org/; an earlier copy read from the Internet Archive), and the Constitution
  amendment Acts as passed and gazetted, National Assembly of Mauritius
  (https://mauritiusassembly.govmu.org/mauritiusassembly/index.php/acts-2/), retrieved 28 October 2026.

The Attorney General's Office states: "Copyright 2011 – Government of Mauritius: As a user of the website, you may
make copies and print out portions of the laws in compliance with the Copyright Act of Mauritius. You should not
publish, disseminate or incorporate, in written or electronic form, the legislations for commercial use without the
prior approval of the Solicitor General." The texts here are reproduced with that attribution and under those terms;
no approval for commercial use has been sought. The National Assembly's site states no licence for the Acts.

Changes made by Lexifina: each version was converted to one Markdown file per provision, one paragraph per line;
spacing, quotes and dashes were normalised; running heads, page numbers and the publisher's bracketed amendment and
editorial notes were left out; and the versions were arranged as git commits credited to the Acts that made each
change, with the words each Act inserts placed where the Act says. The wording of the law was not changed. Neither
the Attorney General's Office nor the National Assembly endorses this repository. Only the texts published by the
Government of Mauritius are authoritative.

Lexifina's own contributions (the arrangement of the history, the commit messages, the tables and the tools) are
dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0 Universal:
https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode
